## 8. Instrumentation requirements

Build a minimal, low-overhead measurement plan before writing an optimization. Prefer existing vLLM metrics/events and add instrumentation only where needed. Ensure instrumentation itself doesn't materially change scheduling or runtime.

For each request/rollout group, capture where possible:

### Request / workload metadata

- question group ID and rollout ID;
- model/adapter/policy version;
- image ID or privacy-safe image content hash;
- original image resolution, processed resolution and crop sizes;
- image/visual-token count, text-prompt token count, generated token count;
- number of requested rollouts and concurrent question groups;
- whether the request is a prewarm, first pass, crop pass, or final answer pass.

### Decoder KV cache

- prefix-cache hit/miss and number of reused prefix tokens/blocks;
- computed/recomputed prefill tokens;
- blocks allocated/freed/retained/evicted;
- eviction reason and whether the block was eligible (e.g., no active references);
- cache occupancy/free-block count over time;
- prefill duration and, if available, layer/phase timing;
- cache lookup/retrieval latency and external load/offload bytes and time.

### Vision encoder cache

- cache hit/miss by multimodal item;
- number of encoder embeddings/slots used and available;
- eviction count and reused embeddings;
- vision-encoder forward count and time;
- image preprocessing time, measured separately from GPU encoder time.

### Whole pipeline

- first-token latency and total rollout latency (median and p95/p99 if sample size permits);
- rollouts/answers per second and questions per second;
- wall-clock seconds per RL step;
- GPU-seconds per rollout and per trained sequence where applicable;
- GPU utilization, GPU memory allocated/reserved, peak memory, CPU memory use;
- CPU↔GPU transfer bytes and latency for offload variants;
- reward/accuracy and output equivalence.

If a field is not exposed, mark it “not available” and explain the instrumentation plan. Do not invent values. Prefer per-step deltas and aggregated counters rather than enormous per-token logs.

