## 9. Candidate solution space to explore (do not select prematurely)

The coding agent should independently reason about and rank these possibilities after baseline profiling. Some may be unnecessary if existing vLLM behavior already addresses the issue.

### A. Rollout-group-aware scheduling / wave size

Schedule rollouts sharing a prefix close together so the shared KV state is more likely to be resident when siblings need it. Limit the number of unrelated question groups admitted at once to control cache pressure. Consider whether a wave-based admission policy or simple group ordering already obtains most of the gain.

Risks: less batching opportunity, idle GPU capacity, fairness/starvation, stragglers when groups have different output lengths, and potential coupling between samples. Tune using end-to-end throughput, not cache hit rate alone.

### B. Prewarm / staged rollout admission

A cheap request can populate the prefix cache before sibling rollouts are submitted. Reproduce the historical one-token prewarm result first. Evaluate whether current vLLM can share concurrent identical-prefix prefill without this, or whether a scheduler/prewarm change is still useful.

Risks: extra model invocation, overhead when the cache already hits, request construction mismatch, and invalid reuse after policy weights change.

### C. Workload-aware retention/admission/eviction

The current prefix cache should be inspected for its exact policy (vLLM's documented prefix-cache design uses hash-indexed blocks, reference counts for active users, and LRU-style eviction among eligible cached blocks, with a longest-prefix/leaf tie-break described in the documentation). Consider whether the rollout workload justifies a more informed policy, such as protecting a prefix shared by several pending sibling rollouts or considering expected reuse probability, recomputation cost, prefix size, and retention horizon.

Possible features for an eviction/retention score:

- expected number of remaining sibling rollouts;
- number of recent/future references to the same question/image prefix;
- decoder prefill cost avoided by retaining the block;
- cache footprint (bytes or token blocks);
- probability that a block will be reused before the end of the policy version;
- time to retrieve from CPU tier versus time to recompute;
- policy/model version and cache validity window.

Do not implement a custom policy until the baseline shows LRU is causing material avoidable recomputation. Current vLLM's **CPU offloading tier** documents support for built-in `lru`/`arc` policies and a custom policy interface in current main; verify whether that path supports the cache scope needed here. A custom offload-tier policy is not the same thing as replacing GPU-resident prefix-cache eviction.

Risks: a “smart” policy can have overhead and make hit rate look better while slowing throughput; overly protected items can crowd out more valuable cache blocks. Include admission control and eviction as distinct decisions if useful.

### D. CPU/off-device KV offloading

Compare retaining blocks on GPU versus offloading completed reusable prefix blocks to pinned CPU memory and loading them back on demand. Test vLLM's current `OffloadingConnector` and/or LMCache before building a custom tier. Consider selective offload of only the long visual prefix / first `N` prompt tokens, leaving low-value generated tails out of the tier if the API permits it.

Compare transfer latency and throughput against simply recomputing the prefix. Offloading is only worthwhile when retrieval is cheaper than recomputation at the relevant concurrency and memory pressure. Secondary storage is slower and should only be explored after CPU tier behavior is understood.

Risks: transfer contention, CPU RAM limits, asynchronous synchronization, connector incompatibility with the model/version/parallelism, block-layout differences, duplicate storage, and cache invalidation after weight updates.

### E. Encoder-output cache policy / budget management

Treat the encoder cache as a separate problem. Determine if the expensive vision tower is being rerun; tune its budget, retention, and request grouping. In the deck's historical test, increasing the encoder cache to 131k (units as configured there) improved throughput by 33%. Replicate, but verify units and current behavior. Avoid spending a lot of effort on the encoder cache if trace evidence says decoder KV prefill is the dominant repeat cost.

### F. Selective prefix caching

Evaluate if the most valuable reusable state is limited to the initial image+question prefix. The generated reasoning/tool/crop suffix often diverges across rollouts and can consume KV space without much cross-rollout reuse. If the runtime supports per-request selective offload/loading or if a cache policy can prioritize the shared prefix, measure this. Do not break prefix hash correctness or assume all multimodal tokens are a single cacheable item.

### G. Reuse of visual embeddings or intermediate representations

The broader project plan mentions reusing visual tokens, potentially selecting a subset/superset, and testing in Hugging Face before porting to vLLM. This is a separate avenue from decoder KV-cache eviction:

- reusing the encoder embeddings can avoid the vision tower;
- reusing decoder KV can avoid decoder prefill;
- changing the visual-token subset or crop resolutions changes the model input and may change accuracy.

For the user's immediate task, start with lossless cache reuse/retention/offload and scheduling. Only explore token subset/superset or lower-resolution approximations if cache-only approaches fail or a clear cost bottleneck justifies them. Any lossy visual-token method needs explicit accuracy/reward evaluation.

### H. Cross-version invalidation and safety

A cache hit is valid only when the cached state corresponds to the same compatible computation. In particular:

- decoder KV computed under old language-model weights cannot be reused after a policy update;
- LoRA/adapter identity and version may need to be part of the cache namespace;
- encoder embeddings can only survive an RL update if every weight and preprocessing component that affects them remains unchanged;
- the current vLLM code has an encoder-cache reset path tied to weight updates; inspect and preserve the correct invalidation semantics;
- crop order, resolution, processor configuration, image identity, positional inputs and multimodal hashes must be respected.

Do not optimize away cache invalidation. Add a regression test that proves cache state from a previous policy version is not used by the next version.

