## 13. Risks and common mistakes

1. **Conflating encoder outputs with decoder KV.** These are different caches and save different computation. Instrument them independently.
2. **Assuming a cache miss means the image encoder reran.** It may only mean decoder prefill was recomputed; verify both paths.
3. **Assuming the slide's old vLLM bug/limitation still exists.** The prewarm observation is a reproduction target, not a timeless fact.
4. **Optimizing hit rate rather than throughput.** A policy can increase hits while making transfers, scheduling or latency worse.
5. **Using an oversized synthetic benchmark only.** Include realistic image resolutions, variable prompt/crop lengths, tool turns and rollouts.
6. **Reusing stale KV after an RL weight update.** This is a correctness bug, not an acceptable performance tradeoff.
7. **Ignoring multimodal hashes and positional details.** Reordering, cropping or changing image processing can make two prompts incompatible even if their text looks similar.
8. **Treating crop outputs as shared.** Different rollouts may select different crops and have divergent suffixes. Only the exact common compatible prefix is reusable.
9. **Jumping straight to DeepSpeed/distributed training.** First prove that repeated rollout computation or cache churn is a material bottleneck. Learn distributed training later if measurements show synchronization/training is dominant.
10. **Reporting stale public API flags as guaranteed.** vLLM and LMCache evolve rapidly; confirm every configuration against the checked-out commit and installed packages.
11. **Claiming novelty before checking prior art.** Native APC, encoder caching, CPU offloading, LMCache and alternate policies must be baseline comparisons.
12. **Accepting lossy visual-token changes as “cache optimizations.”** If the method changes visual inputs, separately measure model quality and call it a quality/compute tradeoff.
13. **Over-instrumenting the hot path.** First measure the instrumentation overhead and avoid per-token logs when counters/timestamps suffice.
14. **Using the wrong memory term.** On the target A100, describe GPU memory/HBM/VRAM rather than SRAM unless discussing actual on-chip SRAM.

